</xref><ext-link xlink:href="https://orcid.org/0000-0001-5783-8048" ext-link-type="uri">0000-0001-5783-8048</ext-link></contrib><aff id="AF0001"><label>1</label><institution content-type="department">Department of Diagnostic Radiology and Nuclear Medicine</institution>, <institution>Kyoto University Hospital</institution>, <addr-line content-type="city">Kyoto</addr-line>, <addr-line content-type="state">Kyoto</addr-line>, <country>Japan</country></aff><aff id="AF0002"><label>2</label><institution content-type="department">Advanced Imaging in Medical Magnetic Resonance</institution>, <institution>Graduate School of Medicine, Kyoto University</institution>, <addr-line content-type="city">Kyoto</addr-line>, <addr-line content-type="state">Kyoto</addr-line>, <country>Japan</country></aff><aff id="AF0003"><label>3</label><institution content-type="department">Department of Gynecology and Obstetrics</institution>, <institution>Graduate School of Medicine, Kyoto University</institution>, <addr-line content-type="city">Kyoto</addr-line>, <addr-line content-type="state">Kyoto</addr-line>, <country>Japan</country></aff><aff id="AF0004"><label>4</label><institution content-type="department">Department of Diagnostic Imaging and Nuclear Medicine</institution>, <institution>Graduate School of Medicine, Kyoto University</institution>, <addr-line content-type="city">Kyoto</addr-line>, <addr-line content-type="state">Kyoto</addr-line>, <country>Japan</country></aff><aff id="AF0005"><label>5</label><institution content-type="department">Department of Diagnostic Pathology</institution>, <institution>Fujita Health University</institution>, <addr-line content-type="city">Toyoake</addr-line>, <addr-line content-type="state">Aichi</addr-line>, <country>Japan</country></aff><aff id="AF0006"><label>6</label><institution content-type="department">Department of Radiology</institution>, <institution>Toyama University Hospital</institution>, <addr-line content-type="city">Toyama</addr-line>, <addr-line content-type="state">Toyama</addr-line>, <country>Japan</country></aff></contrib-group><author-notes><corresp id="COR0001"><label>*</label>Corresponding author: Department of Diagnostic Imaging and Nuclear Medicine, Kyoto University Hospital, 54 Shogoin-kawahara-cho, Sakyo-ku, Kyoto, Kyoto 606-8507, Japan. Phone: 81-75-751-3760, E-mail: <email>yhimoto@kuhp.kyoto-u.ac.jp</email></corresp></author-notes><pub-date pub-type="collection"><year>2025</year></pub-date><pub-date pub-type="epub"><day>06</day><month>2</month><year>2025</year></pub-date><volume>24</volume><issue>3</issue><fpage>343</fpage><lpage>353</lpage><history><date date-type="received"><day>15</day><month>10</month><year>2024</year></date><date date-type="accepted"><day>19</day><month>11</month><year>2024</year></date></history><permissions><copyright-statement>&#x000a9;2025 Japanese Society for Magnetic Resonance in Medicine</copyright-statement><copyright-year>2025</copyright-year><license><ali:license_ref xmlns:ali="http://www.niso.org/schemas/ali/1.0/" specific-use="textmining" content-type="ccbyncndlicense">https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/</ali:license_ref><license-p>This work is licensed under a Creative Commons Attribution-NonCommercial-NoDerivatives 4.0 International License. To view a copy of this license, visit <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/">http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/</ext-link></license-p></license></permissions><abstract><p>Placental insufficiency is a critical condition in perinatal medicine, clinically manifesting as fetal growth restriction or preeclampsia. In addition to ultrasound and Doppler velocimetry, MRI has been assessed intensively for its potential to evaluate placental function directly. Several methods investigated to date include anthropometry, visual assessments using T2-weighted images, and quantitative evaluations based on T2 values, hypoxia indicators (T2* values and blood oxygenation level-dependent imaging), and perfusion metrics (intravoxel incoherent motion and arterial spin labeling). Anthropometry and visual assessments are easily implemented clinically because they require no specific technique or post-processing. By contrast, quantitative approaches provide objective numerical indicators, making them promising imaging biomarkers. Despite their potential, translating these methods into clinical practice presents challenges, especially for quantitative techniques, because of limited availability, lack of standardization, and inadequate clinician awareness. This review was conducted to overview the clinical aspects of placental insufficiency, summarize the anthropometry, visual assessments, and quantitative methods reported, and highlight the latest advancements. It also presents discussion of related challenges and future prospects for clinical implementation.</p></abstract><kwd-group><kwd>fetal growth restriction</kwd><kwd>placental function</kwd><kwd>preeclampsia</kwd></kwd-group></article-meta></front><body><sec id="S0001"><title>Introduction</title><p>The placenta, a multifunctional organ, is essential for the growth, development, and well-being of the fetus. Its key functions include nutrient transfer and gas exchange. Placental insufficiency is a condition by which the placenta fails to perform these functions effectively. Placental insufficiency clinically manifests as fetal growth restriction (FGR) or preeclampsia.<xref rid="R0001" ref-type="bibr"><sup>1</sup></xref><sup>,</sup><xref rid="R0002" ref-type="bibr"><sup>2</sup></xref> Both FGR and preeclampsia are critical conditions in perinatal medicine, presenting severe risks to the life of the fetus and of the mother. According to the developmental origins of the health and disease hypothesis, impaired fetal growth might affect fetal development and short-term and long-term health.<xref rid="R0003" ref-type="bibr"><sup>3</sup></xref><sup>,</sup><xref rid="R0004" ref-type="bibr"><sup>4</sup></xref> In addition to perinatal mortality and morbidity, FGR can engender sequelae and neurological developmental disorders during childhood.<xref rid="R0001" ref-type="bibr"><sup>1</sup></xref> The association of FGR and increased risks of adult chronic diseases including cardiovascular disease, type 2 diabetes, and obesity are also suggested.<xref rid="R0004" ref-type="bibr"><sup>4</sup></xref><sup>,</sup><xref rid="R0005" ref-type="bibr"><sup>5</sup></xref> In cases of preeclampsia, medically induced preterm delivery is often unavoidable, but it entails risks of complications and long-term sequelae associated with prematurity. Frequently, FGR and preeclampsia overlap, leading to severe developmental disabilities and sequelae resulting from preterm birth and FGR. Additionally, preeclampsia remains a leading cause of maternal mortality worldwide. Early detection of placental insufficiency, accurate assessments of the fetal condition, and careful monitoring based on risk stratification must be achieved to minimize these associated risks.</p><p>Placental insufficiency often presents with flattening of the fetal growth curve and decreased movement. In clinical settings, ultrasound is the imaging modality of choice allowing screening for fetal and placental structural abnormalities, biophysical fetal profile, biometry providing estimated fetal weight (EFW) for fetal growth monitoring, and estimation of the amount of amniotic fluid.<xref rid="R0006" ref-type="bibr"><sup>6</sup></xref> Additionally, Doppler velocimetry provides important information to identify pregnancies with signs of placental insufficiency, which can lead to adverse outcomes.<xref rid="R0001" ref-type="bibr"><sup>1</sup></xref><sup>,</sup><xref rid="R0006" ref-type="bibr"><sup>6</sup></xref> However, these findings of ultrasound are limited to indirect evaluation, reflecting fetal growth deviation from the growth curve or maternal/fetal hemodynamic changes secondary to placental insufficiency and fetal hypoxia.<xref rid="R0007" ref-type="bibr"><sup>7</sup></xref> There could be latency between the onset of placental insufficiency and its effect on the measurements of ultrasound. Direct evaluation methods of placental function have not been adopted clinically yet.</p><p>MRI has been studied as an adjunct to ultrasound for the diagnosis of placental abnormalities, particularly addressing placental adhesion disorder. A growing field of investigation is assessing MRI for evaluation of placental function. The modality apparently enables direct and objective evaluation. As an example of that growing attention, among 41 studies and 459 publications funded by the Human Placenta Project of the National Institute of Child Health and Human Development as of May 3, 2023, 16 studies (39%) and 137 publications (30%), including 58 original research publications, were related to MRI.<xref rid="R0008" ref-type="bibr"><sup>8</sup></xref> Furthermore, 52 of 58 original research publications described aspects of placental function.<xref rid="R0008" ref-type="bibr"><sup>8</sup></xref> To date, various methods have been reported, particularly addressing anthropometry, visual assessments, inherent quantitative values, perfusion, and hypoxia.</p><p>These non-invasive MRI methods used to assess placental function are regarded as having promising potential. At present, few findings have been widely recognized, clinically implemented, and considered useful in routine clinical practice. In this review article introducing the latest advancements of placental MRI for the evaluation of placental functions, future challenges and directions for clinical implementations of these MRI methods are discussed.</p></sec><sec id="S0002"><title>Normal MR Images of Placenta</title><p>As a standard of reference, understanding of the normal images of placenta on MRI is important. Clinically, multiplanar 2D rapid T2-weighted images (T2WI) robust to fetal motion artifacts, such as single shot T2WI (ssT2WI; some examples are half-Fourier-acquired single-shot turbo spin echo) and balanced steady-state free precession (bSSFP) are key sequences to evaluate placenta, as well as 3D fat saturated rapid T1-weighted images to detect hemorrhage, and optional diffusion weighted images (DWI).<xref rid="R0009" ref-type="bibr"><sup>9</sup></xref></p><p>On T2WI, placenta is imaged as a discoid organ with high signal intensity (SI). In the second trimester, the placenta is small and thin, showing homogeneous SI (<xref rid="F0001" ref-type="fig">Fig. 1a</xref>).<xref rid="R0010" ref-type="bibr"><sup>10</sup></xref><sup>,</sup><xref rid="R0011" ref-type="bibr"><sup>11</sup></xref> Subsequently, as gestational weeks progress, the placenta becomes larger and thicker, but the SI remains homogeneous in the early third trimester (<xref rid="F0001" ref-type="fig">Fig. 1b</xref>). Its lobules gradually become recognizable and increase in number and clarity.<xref rid="R0010" ref-type="bibr"><sup>10</sup></xref><sup>,</sup><xref rid="R0011" ref-type="bibr"><sup>11</sup></xref> After 36 gestational weeks, stratification of the lobules are more clearly visible (<xref rid="F0001" ref-type="fig">Fig. 1c</xref>).<xref rid="R0010" ref-type="bibr"><sup>10</sup></xref> These lobules are regarded as representing the functional compartments of placenta, called cotyledons.<xref rid="R0011" ref-type="bibr"><sup>11</sup></xref><sup>&#x02013;</sup><xref rid="R0013" ref-type="bibr"><sup>13</sup></xref> More specifically, they appear to be fetal cotyledons (villous trees) or placentones reflecting the concept of a maternal&#x02013;fetal exchange unit,<xref rid="R0014" ref-type="bibr"><sup>14</sup></xref> considering the flow void often recognized in the center of cotyledons on T2WI (<xref rid="F0001" ref-type="fig">Fig. 1c and 1d</xref>). According to the placentone theory, a placentone is one villous tree together with the related part of the intervillous space, where maternal blood inflow comes from a spiral artery in the center and surrounding villous tree.<xref rid="R0014" ref-type="bibr"><sup>14</sup></xref> Histologically, the number of the units in the mature placenta varies considerably, but in most cases, they are between 30 and 50<xref rid="R0014" ref-type="bibr"><sup>14</sup></xref>. On MRI, cotyledon formation is closely associated with gestational aging and placental maturity, similarly to the ultrasonographic grading system of placental maturation.<xref rid="R0010" ref-type="bibr"><sup>10</sup></xref><sup>,</sup><xref rid="R0011" ref-type="bibr"><sup>11</sup></xref><sup>,</sup><xref rid="R0015" ref-type="bibr"><sup>15</sup></xref> About SI on T2WI, the ratio of placental and amniotic fluid SI decreases with higher gestational age, which is concordant to the decrease of T2 value.<xref rid="R0016" ref-type="bibr"><sup>16</sup></xref><sup>,</sup><xref rid="R0017" ref-type="bibr"><sup>17</sup></xref> The transition of the appearance of the placenta over the gestational age is regarded as resulting from the histological maturation of the placenta, such as inter-villous fibrin deposition or villous branching.<xref rid="R0007" ref-type="bibr"><sup>7</sup></xref><sup>,</sup><xref rid="R0010" ref-type="bibr"><sup>10</sup></xref><sup>,</sup><xref rid="R0017" ref-type="bibr"><sup>17</sup></xref> As described in sections &#x0201c;Visual assessment of T2WIs&#x0201d; and &#x0201c;T2 relaxometry,&#x0201d; the relation between placental insufficiency and acceleration of the decrease in T2 values and SI on T2WI have been reported, along with increased visibility of cotyledons.<xref rid="R0007" ref-type="bibr"><sup>7</sup></xref><sup>,</sup><xref rid="R0012" ref-type="bibr"><sup>12</sup></xref><sup>,</sup><xref rid="R0013" ref-type="bibr"><sup>13</sup></xref><sup>,</sup><xref rid="R0016" ref-type="bibr"><sup>16</sup></xref><sup>,</sup><xref rid="R0018" ref-type="bibr"><sup>18</sup></xref></p></sec><sec id="S0003"><title>Placental Insufficiency</title><sec id="S2001"><title>Clinical presentation of placental insufficiency</title><p>Although no standardized definition or consensus exists for placental insufficiency, the general understanding is that it is a process of a progressive deterioration in placental function, and decreased oxygen and nutrient transfer to the fetus, which engenders fetal hypoxia, and also downregulation of fetal metabolic demands to save available nutrients, resulting in FGR, prematurity, or fetal demise.<xref rid="R0019" ref-type="bibr"><sup>19</sup></xref> Placental insufficiency is regarded as a consequence of inadequate remodeling of spiral arteries into highly dilated vessels because of insufficient trophoblast invasion during early pregnancy. Inadequate remodeling can engender increased uteroplacental vascular resistance, and hypoperfusion of the placenta and its downstream effects including coagulation activation, endothelial cell dysfunction, placental thrombosis, and fibrin deposits.<xref rid="R0019" ref-type="bibr"><sup>19</sup></xref> Placental insufficiency is associated with obstetric disorders such as FGR and preeclampsia, both of which predispose to preterm labor, a leading cause of perinatal morbidity and mortality.<xref rid="R0019" ref-type="bibr"><sup>19</sup></xref> The 2 disorders are interrelated and mutually overlap.</p><p>A failure of the fetus to achieve its biological growth potential, FGR, is a common complication of pregnancy, occurring in 5%&#x02013;10% of pregnancies.<xref rid="R0001" ref-type="bibr"><sup>1</sup></xref> The causes of FGR include maternal factors, fetal factors, and factors related to the placenta and umbilical cord. This review will primarily emphasize placental factors. At present, no gold standard exists for diagnosis of FGR. Clinically, it is defined by deviation of the fetal size from a population-based reference, typically using thresholds at the tenth, fifth, or third percentile.<xref rid="R0020" ref-type="bibr"><sup>20</sup></xref> The clinically relevant issue of the definition is the inclusion of constitutionally small but healthy fetuses. It is important to distinguish fetuses with restricted growth because of pathologic factors such as placental insufficiency from small but healthy fetuses among those clinically diagnosed with FGR. Furthermore, variations exist in the definitions of these entities across studies. The definitions should be checked carefully when reviewing the literature. For differentiation between FGR and constitutionally small fetus, monitoring of growth based on sequential ultrasound measurements, Doppler velocimetry, and serum biomarkers play important roles,<xref rid="R0001" ref-type="bibr"><sup>1</sup></xref><sup>,</sup><xref rid="R0020" ref-type="bibr"><sup>20</sup></xref> but no clear-cut method has been established for differentiation; MRI has shown great potential to help it.<xref rid="R0018" ref-type="bibr"><sup>18</sup></xref> At present, no effective treatment exists to reverse or halt the progression of placental insufficiency. For the management of FGR, the main strategies are evaluation of fetal vitality to achieve better growth and lung maturity, and the decision about the timing of delivery to minimize risks of morbidity and mortality.<xref rid="R0001" ref-type="bibr"><sup>1</sup></xref></p><p>Preeclampsia, a progressive disease of pregnancy involving multiple organ systems, is characterized by high blood pressure and often high concentrations of protein in urine, typically after the 20th gestational week (3%&#x02013;5% of pregnancies).<xref rid="R0002" ref-type="bibr"><sup>2</sup></xref> Preeclampsia is hypothesized as a 2-stage disorder.<xref rid="R0021" ref-type="bibr"><sup>21</sup></xref> At stage 1, immunogenic maladaptation and abnormal placentation with inadequate spiral artery remodeling engenders reduction of uteroplacental perfusion. At stage 2, a poorly perfused hypoxic placenta causes a systemic inflammatory response and endothelial dysfunction in the maternal circulatory system, resulting in the maternal syndrome characterized by hypertension and multiorgan injuries, e.g., damage to the kidneys and liver, hemorrhage, stroke, and eclampsia. Its fetal complications include FGR and stillbirth. The only definitive cure for preeclampsia is delivery. The timing depends on the severity and gestational age. For mild cases near term, delivery is usually recommended. For severe cases occurring before the 37th week, it is necessary to determine the timing of delivery while balancing prolongation of pregnancy for fetal growth and minimization of risks to the mother and baby.</p><p>For both disorders, qualitative and quantitative evaluation of placental function by MRI might provide novel information about optimal timing of delivery based on predictions of prognosis and risk stratification.<xref rid="R0018" ref-type="bibr"><sup>18</sup></xref></p></sec><sec id="S2002"><title>Pathology of placental insufficiency</title><p>The pathological condition of maternal vascular malperfusion (MVM) is regarded as the cause of placental insufficiency in pregnancies with FGR, preeclampsia, systemic lupus erythematosus, and antiphospholipid antibody syndrome.<xref rid="R0022" ref-type="bibr"><sup>22</sup></xref> In MVM, altered maternal blood flow to the intervillous space caused by inadequate remodeling of spiral arteries impairs the functional capacity of the placenta.<xref rid="R0022" ref-type="bibr"><sup>22</sup></xref></p><p>Gross findings indicative of MVM are placental hypoplasia and infarction.<xref rid="R0022" ref-type="bibr"><sup>22</sup></xref> Placental hypoplasia is reflected by placental weight that is low for the gestational age. Infarction, which is a common finding of placental insufficiency, is particularly important when observed in a preterm placenta. However, it is noteworthy that infarction can be a normal finding, observed in approximately 25% of normal term pregnancies.<xref rid="R0019" ref-type="bibr"><sup>19</sup></xref> Microscopic findings include abnormalities of villous development (distal villous hypoplasia and accelerated villous maturation), decidual arteriopathy, and placental infarction.<xref rid="R0022" ref-type="bibr"><sup>22</sup></xref> Other findings are increased perivillous fibrin deposition and syncytial knots, and reduced intervillous space.<xref rid="R0022" ref-type="bibr"><sup>22</sup></xref> Many of these histologic findings coexist.</p><p>On clinical MRI, placentas with deteriorated function show diffuse changes on T2WI (low SI and visible cotyledons) as described below, rather than focal lesions such as infarction. Understanding these diffuse microscopic findings, along with recognition that some conditions, influenced by perfusion, can be observed only in utero and might not be reflected in pathological findings, can be crucially important for interpretation of the MRI findings of placental insufficiency.</p></sec><sec id="S2003"><title>Ultrasound</title><p>Ultrasound has been established as a modality of choice to ensure fetal well-being, providing EFW, estimation of the amount of amniotic fluid, and biophysical profile scoring with a nonstress test. Actually, EFW is extremely important for screening and diagnosis of FGR. The fetal growth curve of EFW is a tool to differentiate fetuses with pathologically restricted growth from constitutionally small fetuses with consistent growth percentiles.<xref rid="R0001" ref-type="bibr"><sup>1</sup></xref> Doppler velocimetry, which enables non-invasive detection of signs of hemodynamic changes indirectly reflecting placental insufficiency, can be performed using the umbilical artery for feto-placental vascular resistance, the fetal middle cerebral artery for brain vasodilation, the ductus venosus for fetal hemodynamic centralization, and maternal uterine arteries for maternal circulation. Given the characteristics and severity of each Doppler index, comprehensive assessment of these findings enables detection and risk stratification of fetuses at risk of hypoxia, and optimization of the timing of delivery.</p><p>Figueras and Gratac&#x000f3;s reported a stage-based management protocol based on ultrasound and Doppler findings.<xref rid="R0001" ref-type="bibr"><sup>1</sup></xref><sup>,</sup><xref rid="R0023" ref-type="bibr"><sup>23</sup></xref> Fetuses with EFW between the third and tenth percentiles without Doppler changes were defined as small for gestational age (SGA). FGR was then staged as follows: FGR with EFW below the third percentile with normal Doppler &#x02013; stage 1; FGR with moderate placental insufficiency (with Doppler changes) &#x02013; stage 2; FGR with severe placental insufficiency (Doppler of the umbilical artery with zero diastolic flow) &#x02013; stage 3; FGR with advanced fetal deterioration &#x02013; stage 4; FGR with high probability of fetal acidosis and high risk of fetal death &#x02013; stage 5.<xref rid="R0001" ref-type="bibr"><sup>1</sup></xref><sup>,</sup><xref rid="R0023" ref-type="bibr"><sup>23</sup></xref></p><p>A few studies have examined whether Doppler velocimetry or MRI can detect placental insufficiency earlier. Among pregnancies diagnosed as FGR with the limit of &#x0003c;&#x02212;1.5 standard deviations (SDs) of EFW (approximately the seventh percentile), our study found higher sensitivity of visual assessment using ssT2WI or spin-echo echo-planar imaging (SE-EPI) with TE&#x02009;=&#x02009;60, 90, 120&#x02009;ms (originally for the calculation of T2 values) than absence/reflux of end-diastolic flow of the umbilical artery, which is the definitive Doppler finding of placental insufficiency.<xref rid="R0018" ref-type="bibr"><sup>18</sup></xref> Given use of the direct assessment method, placental MRI might have potential for the early detection of pregnancies with placental insufficiency among pregnancies with SGA or stage 1 FGR. Moreover, MRI might provide additional information about risk stratification among FGR cases staged as 2 and above. Future research from these perspectives is anticipated.</p></sec></sec><sec id="S0004"><title>MRI Evaluation Methods</title><p>Anthropometry, visual assessments, and quantitative evaluation methods for placental function using MRI have been reported (<xref rid="T0001" ref-type="table">Table 1</xref>). Anthropometry and visual assessments are expected to be intuitively easy to understand, and more clinically acceptable because they require no special technique or processing of imaging data. However, quantitative evaluation methods can provide objective numerical indicators or imaging biomarkers for direct placental functional evaluations. The respective methods are discussed below.</p><sec id="S2004"><title>Anthropometry and visual assessments</title><p>The ability to diagnose a placenta as normal and to detect abnormalities based on anthropometry and visual assessments is crucially important for clinical implementation. As detailed below, the 2 approaches indicate that placental insufficiency manifests as a small (but thick) placenta with low SI and visible cotyledons on T2WI, as shown in <xref rid="F0002" ref-type="fig">Fig. 2a and b</xref>.</p></sec><sec id="S0005"><title>Anthropometry</title><p>A normal placenta weighs approximately 400&#x02013;450g. Correlation exists between placental weight at birth and fetal weight. In cases of FGR, the placental weight at birth is lower. By virtue of its large field of view, MRI can visualize the entire placenta clearly. Anthropometric MRI evaluations of the placental function have been studied; evidence has been accumulated. The key findings include volume, thickness, and bulging. Damodaram et al. reported the placental appearance in FGR.<xref rid="R0024" ref-type="bibr"><sup>24</sup></xref> They described that most placentas in FGR were thickened and globular, with decreased volume and with an increased thickness to volume ratio. A study by Ohgiya et al. demonstrated significant differences between insufficient and normal placentas. Their research revealed that insufficient placentas were characterized by increased thickness (4.9&#x02009;cm compared to 3.7&#x02009;cm), yet had smaller areas (162 cm<sup>2</sup> versus 267 cm<sup>2</sup>) and reduced volumes (513&#x02009;cm<sup>3</sup> in contrast to 656 cm<sup>3</sup>) when compared to their normal counterparts.<xref rid="R0025" ref-type="bibr"><sup>25</sup></xref> Among pregnancies diagnosed as FGR, we reported that pregnancies with unfavorable outcomes showed smaller placental size (major axis, minor axis, estimated area of placental bed, and volume of placenta [258&#x02009;cm<sup>3</sup> (89&#x02013;540) vs 425&#x02009;cm<sup>3</sup> (266&#x02013;682)]) than pregnancies with favorable outcome, although the respective placental thicknesses did not differ.<xref rid="R0018" ref-type="bibr"><sup>18</sup></xref> It is noteworthy that not all placentas with placental insufficiency show prominent bulging or thickening, perhaps because their originally small size caused by abnormal placentation or rapid deterioration outpaces compensatory changes. Ho et al. reported that the placental volume in the preeclamptic group was not significantly different to the placental volume in the gestation-matched control group.<xref rid="R0026" ref-type="bibr"><sup>26</sup></xref> It is particularly interesting that the delivered placenta is sometimes very different from the placenta in utero because it is no longer inflated by maternal blood.<xref rid="R0007" ref-type="bibr"><sup>7</sup></xref> In a clinical setting, it is not infrequently observed that a placenta that appears thickened and bulging on MRI is found to be very small and flattened after delivery (<xref rid="F0002" ref-type="fig">Fig. 2d</xref>).</p><p>As in other areas, research using artificial intelligence (AI) has been advancing in placental MRI. Kulseng et al. developed a convolutional neural network that enables rapid and accurate placental and fetal volume estimation.<xref rid="R0027" ref-type="bibr"><sup>27</sup></xref> Automated placental segmentation with AI might facilitate quicker, more objective, and more accurate measurements, enhancing the usefulness of anthropometry. Consideration of the gestational age and respective disease condition (e.g., speed of disease progression, early or late onset of FGR/preeclampsia) and objective automatic measurements can be expected to improve the clinical utility of anthropometric MRI methods.</p></sec><sec id="S0006"><title>Visual assessment of T2WIs</title><p>In contrast to a turbo/fast spin echo T2WI, which is used for gynecologic imaging, ssT2WI is a key sequence for placental MRI because of its rapidity and robustness to motion artifacts. Association of the visibility of cotyledons on ssT2WI and placental function has been reported. As the pregnancy progresses, the placental functional units called cotyledons become more noticeable, reflecting placental maturation.<xref rid="R0010" ref-type="bibr"><sup>10</sup></xref><sup>,</sup><xref rid="R0011" ref-type="bibr"><sup>11</sup></xref> We reported 2 cases of preeclampsia and of twin pregnancy with 1 fetus having FGR, suggesting a relation between low SI placenta with visible cotyledons on ssT2WI and placental insufficiency.<xref rid="R0012" ref-type="bibr"><sup>12</sup></xref> We also reported a visual scoring system using ssT2WI for placental insufficiency based on the degree of low SI of the placenta and visualization of cotyledons. Compared to score 1 with higher SI than that of fetal kidney, scores 2 or 3 with lower SI than that of fetal kidney or liver were associated with the pathologically proven placental insufficiency and SGA neonates.<xref rid="R0013" ref-type="bibr"><sup>13</sup></xref> We hypothesized that diffusely lower SI in placenta represents the aggregation of <italic>in vivo</italic> compensatory alterations in the respective cotyledons caused by placental hypo-circulation, i.e., alterations of the distribution of villus, maternal blood, and its oxygen saturation.<xref rid="R0013" ref-type="bibr"><sup>13</sup></xref> Specifically examining pregnancies diagnosed as FGR &#x0003c;&#x02212;1.5 SD of EFW before gestational week 37, we reported visual analysis of ssT2WI and SE-EPI with TE&#x02009;=&#x02009;60, 90, and 120 for creation of a T2 map based on the visibility of cotyledons. The analysis would facilitate risk stratification regarding unfavorable outcomes such as intrauterine fetal death or emergent caesarean section before term (<xref rid="F0003" ref-type="fig">Figs. 3</xref> and <xref rid="F0004" ref-type="fig">4</xref>).<xref rid="R0018" ref-type="bibr"><sup>18</sup></xref> Considering that ssT2WI is a key sequence in placental MRI and also considering that SE-EPI is identical to DWI with <italic>b</italic>&#x02009;=&#x02009;0, clinical implementation of visual evaluation of placental function is expected to be feasible.
</p><p>For pregnancies complicated by preeclampsia, Ho et al. reported similar findings to ours on ssT2WI along with T2* relaxometry: marked lobularity, high SI in the center of lobules corresponding to long T2*, and peripheral low SI of placental parenchyma corresponding to short T2* values, which would be visualized as cotyledons.<xref rid="R0026" ref-type="bibr"><sup>26</sup></xref> These diffuse findings on T2WI likely reflect diffuse pathophysiological changes of placental insufficiency in utero, rather than a focal lesion such as infarction.</p></sec><sec id="S2005"><title>Quantitative evaluation</title><p>Quantification of key factors related to placental function using MRI has been emphasized recently. Various methods particularly addressing the estimation of pathophysiological alterations, oxygenations, and perfusion have been explored. Although many challenges remain for clinical application (e.g., complexity of image acquisition and post-processing, underutilization, and standardization), these techniques present the promising potential to provide objective indicators for diagnosis, and for the monitoring of disease progression or treatment effects of placental insufficiency. These MRI methods are introduced respectively below.</p></sec><sec id="S0007"><title>T2 relaxometry</title><p>As its name suggests, T2 relaxometry enables quantitative measurement of the T2 relaxation time of tissues: the time constant of how quickly the spins of hydrogen nuclei in the tissue lose coherence (or diphase) after excitation by a radio frequency pulse. The T2 relaxation times or T2 values are calculable from a series of MR scans with varying TEs. These values are regarded as reflecting tissue-specific characteristics. The T2 value of normal placenta is known to decrease as pregnancy progresses, possibly reflecting placental maturation.<xref rid="R0016" ref-type="bibr"><sup>16</sup></xref><sup>,</sup><xref rid="R0017" ref-type="bibr"><sup>17</sup></xref> Acceleration of the decrease of T2 values in pregnancies with placental insufficiency has been reported. Derwig et al. reported an association between a decrease of placental T2 values and an increase of uterine artery pulsatility index on Doppler ultrasound, and subsequent delivery of SGA neonates.<xref rid="R0028" ref-type="bibr"><sup>28</sup></xref> Among pregnancies diagnosed as FGR, using a 1.5T MRI scanner, we reported that T2 mapping based histogram features are significantly different between those of pregnancies with and without unfavorable outcomes: FGR with unfavorable outcomes showed lower mean T2 values (mean [range], 103&#x02009;ms [91&#x02013;130] vs 123&#x02009;ms [97&#x02013;160]), higher skewness (concentration on the left), and higher kurtosis (a sharp peak).<xref rid="R0018" ref-type="bibr"><sup>18</sup></xref> We infer that these differences indicate the severity of placental insufficiency, reflecting compensatory microstructural changes and subsequent unfavorable prognosis<xref rid="R0018" ref-type="bibr"><sup>18</sup></xref>, which would enable risk stratification of pregnancies clinically diagnosed as FGR (<xref rid="F0003" ref-type="fig">Figs. 3</xref> and <xref rid="F0004" ref-type="fig">4</xref>). Because T2 values are also affected by the level of oxygen saturation (higher oxygen saturation result in higher T2 values), the decrease of T2 values might provide some information about feto-placental oxygenation.<xref rid="R0029" ref-type="bibr"><sup>29</sup></xref> Considering the decrease of T2 values of placentas with the increase of gestational age, a normalization technique (e.g., Z score) accounting for that decrease would be preferred for clinical implementation.<xref rid="R0016" ref-type="bibr"><sup>16</sup></xref></p></sec><sec id="S0008"><title>T2* relaxometry</title><p>For the evaluation of placental insufficiency, quantitative assessments of hypoxic conditions have been a primary focus. For measurement of this condition, T2* relaxometry has been applied. T2* values and R2* (1/T2*) are calculable from a series of gradient echo MR scans with multiple TEs, similarly to the measurement of T2 values. Theoretically, deoxyhemoglobin is more paramagnetic than oxyhemoglobin, which causes magnetic field distortions and faster T2* decay (reduced transverse relaxation time). Therefore, hypoxic tissues have a reduced T2* value (blood oxygen level dependency effect). It is noteworthy that the T2* values are inherently affected by the magnetic field strength (i.e., shorter at a higher field). Similarly to T2 values, decreased T2* values over gestation were observed. Additionally, similarly to T2WI, a decline in T2* value from center to periphery was found in the respective placental lobules, which the authors speculated as representing oxygen exchange between maternal and fetal circulation within the intervillous space.<xref rid="R0030" ref-type="bibr"><sup>30</sup></xref></p><p>Compared to normal placentas, lower T2* values in placentas with various pathophysiological conditions have been reported: FGR,<xref rid="R0031" ref-type="bibr"><sup>31</sup></xref><sup>&#x02013;</sup><xref rid="R0033" ref-type="bibr"><sup>33</sup></xref> preeclampsia,<xref rid="R0026" ref-type="bibr"><sup>26</sup></xref> and congenital fetal heart disease.<xref rid="R0034" ref-type="bibr"><sup>34</sup></xref> Regarding FGR, using a 3.0T MRI scanner, He et al. reported that T2* values (Z score) of pregnancies with EFW&#x02009;&#x0003c;&#x02009;the third percentile were significantly lower than those of pregnancies with EFW&#x02009;&#x0003c;&#x02009;the tenth percentile (the raw T2* values, 28.23&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;12.33&#x02009;ms vs 45.04&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;19.26&#x02009;ms; Z score, &#x02212;3.181&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;1.609 vs &#x02212;1.483&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;1.378), although diffusion and intravoxel incoherent motion (IVIM) parameters (apparent diffusion coefficient [ADC]; <italic>f, D, D</italic>*) did not differ.<xref rid="R0031" ref-type="bibr"><sup>31</sup></xref> They concluded that the most distinct differences between the 2 groups would be insufficient exchange of oxygen between the mother and fetus, rather than hypoperfusion of the placenta.<xref rid="R0031" ref-type="bibr"><sup>31</sup></xref> Similarly, Ho et al. reported that the overall placental mean T2* of patients with preeclampsia were lower than those of gestation-matched normal pregnancies (median, 23&#x02009;ms vs 67&#x02009;ms at 26.0 to 29.8&#x02009;weeks&#x02019; gestation; 22&#x02009;ms vs 38&#x02009;ms at 34.0&#x02013;38.9&#x02009;weeks&#x02019; gestation), using a 3.0T MRI scanner. In addition, the mean T2* values of placentas with preeclampsia remained low in the time course, whereas those of normal pregnancies decreased.<xref rid="R0026" ref-type="bibr"><sup>26</sup></xref></p><p>Because the previously described T2 values and T2* values show similar behaviors, such as decreases attributable to the influences of gestational age and placental insufficiency, it is unclear whether the decrease in T2* is purely attributable to the effect of hypoxia. Comparison between T2 values and T2* values might provide additional information, more particularly addressing hypoxic conditions, in the future. Furthermore, when interpreting results of T2* values, it is noteworthy that T2* values fundamentally reflect field inhomogeneities other than deoxyhemoglobin (placental tissue morphology including histological maturation).<xref rid="R0029" ref-type="bibr"><sup>29</sup></xref></p></sec><sec id="S0009"><title>Blood oxygenation level-dependent MRI</title><p>Blood oxygenation level-dependent (BOLD) MRI is a method to detect changes in the deoxyhemoglobin concentrations of tissues after respiration challenges, such as oxygen administration, using a gradient echo sequence. Actually, T2* values cannot be directly related to tissue oxygenation because tissue morphology also affects them (e.g., placental maturation in placental MRI).<xref rid="R0029" ref-type="bibr"><sup>29</sup></xref> The difference in the R2* values (delta R2*), which is calculable from T2* weighted images before and after a respiration challenge, can represent the changes in oxygenation independent of baseline conditions.<xref rid="R0029" ref-type="bibr"><sup>29</sup></xref> In placental MRI, it has been used to measure changes in placental and fetal brain oxygenation by maternal oxygen administration in normal pregnancies, pregnancies complicated by FGR, or by fetal congenital heart disease.<xref rid="R0033" ref-type="bibr"><sup>33</sup></xref><sup>&#x02013;</sup><xref rid="R0036" ref-type="bibr"><sup>36</sup></xref> The BOLD effect in response to maternal oxygen administration has been reported to be visible.<xref rid="R0033" ref-type="bibr"><sup>33</sup></xref> Sinding et al. reported that pregnancies complicated by placental insufficiency showed higher BOLD effects.<xref rid="R0035" ref-type="bibr"><sup>35</sup></xref> Magawa et al. reported that the peak of delta R2* of pregnancies complicated by FGR (5.52s<sup>&#x02212;1</sup>&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;1.36) was smaller than that of normal pregnancies (7.99s<sup>&#x02212;1</sup>&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;2.58).<xref rid="R0036" ref-type="bibr"><sup>36</sup></xref> Jacquier et al. reported no significant difference of relative BOLD effect between pregnancies with and without FGR.<xref rid="R0033" ref-type="bibr"><sup>33</sup></xref> That report described that their differences might be attributable to differences in the selection of pregnancies of FGR.<xref rid="R0033" ref-type="bibr"><sup>33</sup></xref> It is noteworthy that Abaci Turk et al. reported the effects of maternal position and uterine contraction on BOLD measurements.<xref rid="R0037" ref-type="bibr"><sup>37</sup></xref> Future standardization of the methodology and verification of the repeatability and reproducibility of BOLD measurements, as well as other quantitative measurements, are desirable for clinical implementation.</p></sec><sec id="S0010"><title>DWIs and perfusion images</title><p>DWIs, which are sensitive to water motion and which might include intra-cellular and extra-cellular water diffusion, have been clinically implemented in most MRI scanners and have been optionally performed in clinical placental MRI. ADC is calculated from DWI, which enables quantification of the degree of restricted diffusion of water molecules in tissues. The utility of DWI for assessing placental function has been investigated. Several studies have presented trends for decreases in diffusivity with increasing gestational age and placental insufficiency.<xref rid="R0029" ref-type="bibr"><sup>29</sup></xref><sup>,</sup><xref rid="R0038" ref-type="bibr"><sup>38</sup></xref><sup>,</sup><xref rid="R0039" ref-type="bibr"><sup>39</sup></xref> Bonel et al. reported an association between reduced ADC and placental dysfunction (1.46&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;0.10 10<sup>&#x02212;3</sup> mm<sup>2</sup>/s vs 1.77&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;0.19 10<sup>&#x02212;3</sup> mm<sup>2</sup>/s), and that the addition of DWI to ultrasound improved sensitivity for the detection of placental insufficiency.<xref rid="R0040" ref-type="bibr"><sup>40</sup></xref> Additionally, echo planar images with a <italic>b</italic> value of 0 might offer some potential for use in visual assessments of placental insufficiency, as described in the section &#x0201c;Visual assessment of T2WIs.&#x0201d;</p><p>Alternatively, IVIM is a technique to separate the diffusion of water molecules and perfusion effects within a voxel using a multiple <italic>b</italic> value approach, providing perfusion fraction (f), a true diffusion coefficient (D or D<sub>slow</sub>), and a pseudo-diffusion coefficient (D* or D<sub>fast</sub>). For placental MRI, IVIM has been explored as a contrast-free evaluation of placental perfusion. Some studies have demonstrated an association between IVIM parameters and FGR. We have described the association between f, D*, and adverse obstetric outcomes, as well as T2 values.<xref rid="R0016" ref-type="bibr"><sup>16</sup></xref> Pregnancies with small for gestational age fetuses showed smaller f, compared with those of pregnancies with appropriate gestational age fetuses (23.5% vs 27.0%). We also demonstrated that IVIM parameters were not associated significantly with gestational age.<xref rid="R0016" ref-type="bibr"><sup>16</sup></xref> Similarly, Liu et al. reported placental f as significantly lower in pregnancies diagnosed with FGR (33.96&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;2.62%), compared to normal pregnancies (38.48 &#x000b1;&#x02009;5.31%), and reported that f increased moderately with gestational age in normal pregnancies.<xref rid="R0041" ref-type="bibr"><sup>41</sup></xref> As described above, He et al. reported that IVIM parameters (f, D, and D*) in pregnancies with EFW&#x02009;&#x0003c;&#x02009;the third percentile did not differ significantly from those in pregnancies with EFW&#x02009;&#x0003c;&#x02009;the tenth percentile.<xref rid="R0031" ref-type="bibr"><sup>31</sup></xref> Further investigation is necessary to enhance and extend the clinical applications of IVIM.</p><p>Aside from IVIM, various techniques have been considered for the evaluation of perfusion of placenta. For animals, dynamic contrast-enhanced (DCE) MRI can be an option. DCE-MRI demonstrated reduced perfusion in pregnant rats and mouse models of preeclampsia and growth restriction. In human placenta, seeking exogenous contrast-free perfusion imaging, arterial spin labeling (ASL) has been applied, using arterial blood water as an endogenous tracer by modifying its longitudinal magnetization with RF pulses and gradient fields.<xref rid="R0042" ref-type="bibr"><sup>42</sup></xref> Several labeling methods have been assessed, such as pulsed ASL, (pseudo)-continuous ASL (CASL/pCASL), and velocity-selective ASL (VS-ASL).<xref rid="R0042" ref-type="bibr"><sup>42</sup></xref> In placental ASL, the major path of maternal arterial blood flow to the placenta is via the descending aorta, internal iliac arteries, and uterine arteries (and ovarian arteries). Using pCASL with a labeling plane placed at a bifurcation of the descending aorta, Liu et al. reported significantly lower placental perfusion in high-risk pregnancies than in healthy pregnancies in the center of the placental lobules, where perfusion is high (high placental blood flow, 180.7 mL/100&#x02009;g/min vs 278.1&#x02009;mL/100&#x02009;g/min).<xref rid="R0043" ref-type="bibr"><sup>43</sup></xref> A VS-ASL is another approach. It makes the signal sensitive to the movement of blood, even within the placenta, and is influenced by both maternal and fetal blood flow. Zun et al. reported a significant decrease of placental perfusion with advancing gestational age in pregnancies with fetal heart disease, while not observed in healthy pregnancies.<xref rid="R0044" ref-type="bibr"><sup>44</sup></xref> Hutter et al. reported simultaneous measurements of perfusion and oxygenation by the combination of VS-ASL and T2* mapping.<xref rid="R0045" ref-type="bibr"><sup>45</sup></xref> Although placental ASL techniques are developed on a work-in-progress sequences and although they are challenging to apply in clinical practice because of their vulnerability to motion, they can provide direct measurement of perfusion.<xref rid="R0029" ref-type="bibr"><sup>29</sup></xref></p></sec></sec><sec id="S0011"><title>Challenges and Future Directions</title><p>At present, several barriers persist in hindering the translation of MRI studies to clinical settings. These include needs for specific imaging techniques and post-processing for quantitative evaluations, insufficient evidence supporting them as a potential clinical issue-solving modality (with added value beyond what ultrasound offers), and lack of awareness among clinicians. Verification of the robustness of quantitative MRI measurements is also mandatory. The Quantitative Imaging Biomarkers Alliance (QIBA), an organization dedicated to standardizing and improving the utility of quantitative imaging biomarkers, assigns importance to standardization and assurance of the robustness of imaging biomarkers, along with validation of their utility. Because many techniques are in the exploratory phase of clinical research and are not widespread, it remains difficult to conduct multi-center studies. The next step for the future clinical implementation is the gradual increase of the number of institutions adopting the techniques, building evidence through multi-center studies, and sharing the findings with clinicians such as radiologists, obstetricians, and pathologists.</p><p>Automation of processes of obtaining quantitative measurements is also a key to clinical implementation, consequently improving repeatability and reproducibility and saving time, especially for segmentation of placenta. Kulseng et al. reported a deep learning algorithm to estimate placental and fetal volumes based on bSSFP.<xref rid="R0027" ref-type="bibr"><sup>27</sup></xref> The algorithm reduced the time spent for necessary volume estimation from 60&#x02013;90&#x02009;mins to 10s.</p><p>Another step for the future would be emphases on specific clinical conditions and their associated issues. For example, FGR is typically divided into early onset and late onset, most frequently defined as diagnosis before or after 32nd week of gestation. The 2 designations have different clinical phenotypes. Early onset FGR has high fetal hypoxia and high perinatal morbidity and mortality rates. Consequently, the major clinical challenge is management. By contrast, late onset FGR shows mild hypoxia. Moreover, it is difficult to detect because Doppler findings might be normal. The role of placental MRI might vary depending on each condition. The integration of other methods to evaluate placental function and fetal status is also expected to be important: e.g., various Doppler indexes or circulating placenta-associated biomarkers. Continued exploration of how unmet needs in each clinical scenario can be addressed using placental MRI is expected to be important.</p></sec><sec id="S0012"><title>Conclusion</title><p>Evidence supporting the utility of MRI for placental insufficiency has been accumulating. Although many challenges must be addressed, such as availability and repeatability/reproducibility, resolving these issues step-by-step will lead to greater and more beneficial future clinical implementations.</p></sec></body><back><fn-group><fn fn-type="COI-statement"><p><bold>Conflicts of interest:</bold> The authors declare that they have no conflict of interest.</p></fn></fn-group><ref-list><title>References</title><ref id="R0001"><label>1.</label><mixed-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Nardozza</surname><given-names>LM</given-names></name><name><surname>Caetano</surname><given-names>AC</given-names></name><name><surname>Zamarian</surname><given-names>AC</given-names></name><etal/></person-group>
