<year>2020</year>; <volume>51</volume>:<page-range>1247&#x02013;1257</page-range>.<pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1002/jmri.26944</pub-id></mixed-citation></ref><ref id="R0044"><label>44.</label><mixed-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Zun</surname><given-names>Z</given-names></name><name><surname>Zaharchuk</surname><given-names>G</given-names></name><name><surname>Andescavage</surname><given-names>NN</given-names></name><name><surname>Donofrio</surname><given-names>MT</given-names></name><name><surname>Limperopoulos</surname><given-names>C.</given-names></name></person-group>
<article-title>Non-invasive placental perfusion imaging in pregnancies complicated by fetal heart disease using velocity-selective arterial spin labeled MRI</article-title>. <source>Sci Rep</source>
<year>2017</year>; <volume>7</volume>:<page-range>16126</page-range>.<pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1038/s41598-017-16461-8</pub-id></mixed-citation></ref><ref id="R0045"><label>45.</label><mixed-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Hutter</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Harteveld</surname><given-names>AA</given-names></name><name><surname>Jackson</surname><given-names>LH</given-names></name><etal/></person-group>
<article-title>Perfusion and apparent oxygenation in the human placenta (PERFOX)</article-title>. <source>Magn Reson Med</source>
<year>2020</year>; <volume>83</volume>:<page-range>549&#x02013;560</page-range>.<pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1002/mrm.27950</pub-id></mixed-citation></ref></ref-list></back><floats-group><fig position="float" id="F0001" fig-type="figure"><label>Fig. 1</label><caption><p>Three examples of single shot T2-weighted images of normal placentas. In the second trimester, the placenta is small and thin, showing homogeneous high signal intensity (<bold>a</bold>, at the 20th gestational week). As gestation progresses, the placenta becomes larger and thicker, but remains homogeneous in the early third trimester (<bold>b</bold>, at the 30th gestational week). In the late third trimester, its lobules gradually become recognizable, as represented by the functional compartments called cotyledons (<bold>c</bold>, at the 36th gestational week). In <bold>d</bold> (same as <bold>c</bold>), flow voids of spiral arteries (arrows) are visible in the center of cotyledons (dotted circles).</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="mrms-24-343-g1" position="float"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F0002" fig-type="figure"><label>Fig. 2</label><caption><p>A representative case of placental insufficiency, complicated with fetal growth restriction (estimated fetal weight &#x0003c; &#x02212;2.5 SD). At the 32nd gestational week, axial single shot T2-weighted images (<bold>a</bold> and <bold>b</bold>, same images) show small but thick placenta (64&#x02009;mm thickness) with low SI and visible cotyledons. In <bold>b</bold>, arrows indicate flow voids of spiral arteries, and dotted circles indicate cotyledons. Sagittal spin-echo echo-planar images with TE&#x02009;=&#x02009;90 (for creation of the T2 map) clearly illustrate diffuse low SI with visible cotyledons (<bold>c</bold>, arrows: high SI in the center of lobules). Fourteen days after MRI (at the 34th gestational week), emergent caesarean section was done because of the reduced fetal movements. The delivered placenta (<bold>d</bold>) was small (190g, &#x0003c;tenth percentile) and much thinner (22&#x02009;mm thickness) than shown by the MRI, which was regarded as reflecting compensatory inflation by maternal blood. Pathological examination revealed conditions of maternal vascular malperfusion. SI, signal intensity; SD, standard deviation.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="mrms-24-343-g2" position="float"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F0003" fig-type="figure"><label>Fig. 3</label><caption><p>A representative case of a pregnancy clinically diagnosed fetal growth restriction, but successfully delivered at the full term. At the 25th gestational week, sagittal single shot T2-weighted images (<bold>a</bold>) and spin-echo echo-planar images with TE&#x02009;=&#x02009;90 (<bold>b</bold>, for T2 map creation) show the small and thin placenta with homogeneous high signal intensity (29&#x02009;mm thickness). Cotyledons were not visible in either sequence, which is normal for the gestational age. The T2 map of the placenta (<bold>c</bold>) is homogeneous and bright, representing high T2 values.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="mrms-24-343-g3" position="float"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F0004" fig-type="figure"><label>Fig. 4</label><caption><p>A case of a pregnancy clinically diagnosed fetal growth restriction that developed preeclampsia 2 days after the MRI. At the 24th gestational week, coronal single shot T2-weighted images (<bold>a</bold>) and spin-echo echo-planar images with TE&#x02009;=&#x02009;90 (<bold>b</bold>, for the creation of T2 map) show the small placenta (33&#x02009;mm thickness) with low signal intensity and visible cotyledon on both sequences. The T2 map of the placenta is dark, representing lower T2 values. At 16&#x02009;days after MRI (at the 26th gestational week), partial hemolysis, elevated liver enzymes, and low platelets (HELLP) syndrome was diagnosed; emergent caesarean section was done.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="mrms-24-343-g4" position="float"/></fig><table-wrap position="float" id="T0001"><label>Table 1</label><caption><p>Anthropometry, visual assessments, and quantitative MRI methods for the evaluation of placental function</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><thead><tr><th align="left" valign="middle" rowspan="1" colspan="1">MRI methods</th><th valign="middle" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Focus</th><th valign="middle" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Parameter/feature</th><th valign="middle" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Placental insufficiency</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td align="left" valign="middle" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Anthropometry</td><td align="left" valign="middle" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Structure</td><td align="left" valign="middle" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Volume, thickness, shape</td><td align="left" valign="middle" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Small volume, increase in thickness, globular shape</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="middle" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Visual assessments</td><td valign="middle" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td valign="middle" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td valign="middle" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="middle" rowspan="1" colspan="1">T2-weighted images</td><td align="left" valign="middle" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Structure and pathophysiology</td><td align="left" valign="middle" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Signal intensity, visibility of cotyledons</td><td align="left" valign="middle" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Decrease in signal intensity, increase in the visibility of cotyledons</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="middle" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Quantitative</td><td valign="middle" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td valign="middle" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td valign="middle" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="middle" rowspan="1" colspan="1">T2 relaxometry</td><td align="left" valign="middle" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Pathophysiology, oxygenation</td><td align="left" valign="middle" rowspan="1" colspan="1">T2 value</td><td align="left" valign="middle" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Decrease</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="middle" rowspan="1" colspan="1">T2* relaxometry</td><td align="left" valign="middle" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Pathophysiology, oxygenation</td><td align="left" valign="middle" rowspan="1" colspan="1">T2* value</td><td align="left" valign="middle" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Decrease</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="middle" rowspan="1" colspan="1">BOLD</td><td align="left" valign="middle" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Oxygenation change after a respiration challenge</td><td align="left" valign="middle" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x00394;R2*</td><td align="left" valign="middle" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Arguable and requires further evidence</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="middle" rowspan="1" colspan="1">DWI</td><td align="left" valign="middle" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Diffusivity of water, microstructure</td><td align="left" valign="middle" rowspan="1" colspan="1">ADC</td><td align="left" valign="middle" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Decrease</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="middle" rowspan="1" colspan="1">IVIM</td><td align="left" valign="middle" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Perfusion, diffusivity of water</td><td align="left" valign="middle" rowspan="1" colspan="1">f, D(D<sub>slow</sub>), D*(D<sub>fast</sub>)</td><td align="left" valign="middle" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Decrease in f and D*(D<sub>fast</sub>)</td></tr><tr><td align="left" valign="middle" rowspan="1" colspan="1">ASL</td><td align="left" valign="middle" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Perfusion, blood flow</td><td align="left" valign="middle" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Blood flow, arterial transit time</td><td align="left" valign="middle" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Decrease in high placental blood flow</td></tr></tbody></table><table-wrap-foot><p>ADC, apparent diffusion coefficient; ASL, arterial spin labeling; BOLD, blood oxygenation level-dependent; DWI, diffusion weighted image; IVIM, intravoxel incoherent motion.</p></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap></floats-group></article>